			<div class="content" id="post-content-2331417">
									

<p>Nope. It wouldn't have exploded until it contacted the lunar surface. The density of interplanetary space in the vicinity of Earth is approximately five particles per cubic centimeter. In order to annihilate, there needs to be one particle for every antiparticle present.</p>
<p>Let's try a little thought experiment.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">MATH&nbsp;MATH&nbsp;MATH</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">WOO&nbsp;CANCELING&nbsp;UNITS</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>An ellipsoid the same size as the damaged alien vessel would have a volume of 32044.25&#160;m<sup>3</sup> or 3204425&#160;cm<sup>3</sup>. For the sake of simplicity, let's say our test ellipsoid is solid and made of low alloy steel. Low alloy steel has a density of 7.60&#160;g/cm<sup>3</sup>. So our ellipsoid has a mass of 24353630&#160;g. One gram of iron has roughly 1.1x10<sup>22</sup> iron atoms. And an individual iron atom (let's go with the most common iron isotope, <sup>56</sup>Fe) is composed of 82 particles (30 neutrons, 26 protons, 26 electrons).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>So, roughly, the total number of particles in our test ellipsoid is about (24353630&#160;g) x (1.1x10<sup>22</sup> atoms/g) x(82 particles/atom) = 2.1966974 x 10<sup>31</sup> particles or 21,966,974,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000 particles.</p>
<p>The same volume of interplanetary space would have about 16,022,125 particles. The alien vessel would degrade over time as it moved through the interplanetary medium, but it would take a very, very, very long time to be destroyed. The effect within the article's timeline is negligible.</p>

							</div>

								
