<p>I'm more concerned about the damaged vessel hitting the moon - the article listed it as being in the kT range, but some quick math suggests that&#8230; No, it's gonna be bigger.</p>
<p>Let's see: assuming 100% matter to energy conversion, that gives us 24,353.63 Kg * c<sup>2</sup> = 2.1887951 * 10<sup>21</sup> Joules, or about a 0.5 teraton explosion. Yoikes.</p>
<p>Now, what we're all wondering: will this blow up the moon? &#8230;Not quite.</p>
<p>The gravitational binding energy of the moon is estimated at ~10<sup>29</sup> Joules, or 8 orders of magnitude greater than our antimatter bomb-ship can provide even at 100% conversion.</p>
<p>It'd leave a hell of a crater, though.</p>

