<p>Alright, you got me there. I think I got my kilotons and megatons confused, but the explosion would be well within the gigatons. There's a decent amount of wiggle room with the test mass I used, since the vessel already had chunks missing and had interior space, so I shoved the explosive power up to 430 gigatons (.43 teratons).</p>
<p>The size of the crater should be fine. For comparison, the Chicxulub crater is 180&#160;km in width and 20&#160;km in depth, and the energy of the impact that created it is around 100 teratons. That's a big boom.</p>
<p>Some calculations I did with an impact energy calculator showed my crater is well within the ballpark for a 430 gigaton boom.</p>

