<p>Sorry for necroposting, but that m<sup>3</sup> to cm<sup>3</sup> conversion is wrong.<br />
1&#160;m = 100&#160;cm, so 1&#160;m<sup>3</sup> = 100<sup>3</sup> cm<sup>3</sup> = 1000000&#160;cm<sup>3</sup>.<br />
The rest was right, you just forgot to multiply by 10000.<br />
243536300&#160;kg * c<sup>2</sup> = 2.18879511*10<sup>25</sup> J = 5.2313459 <em>petatons</em> of TNT.</p>
<p><a href="http://www.wolframalpha.com/input/?i=convert+(4%2F3*pi*30*17*15)*7.60*10%5E6+grams+to+tons+of+TNT">WolframAlpha says</a> that's ~44 times the energy of the impact that caused <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicxulub_crater" onclick="window.open(this.href, '_blank'); return false;">the Chicxulub crater</a>, which is 180km wide and 20km deep.<br />
Even with your rounded-down estimate, <a href="http://www.wolframalpha.com/input/?i=4.3*10%5E15+tons+of+TNT">it's still ~36 Chicxulubs</a>.<br />
I don't know much about explosions, but I'm not sure the moon would survive that.</p>

