					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ooblex" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2164005); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2164005&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606447554" alt="ooblex" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2164005)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ooblex" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2164005); return false;" >ooblex</a></span> <span class="odate time_1436570848 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Jul 2015 23:27</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2335839">
									

<p>I find it kind of difficult to believe that the entire interior of the ship could be seen from many angles through a one-foot-wide window. Also, why was the hatch made of some kind of glass (sorry, anti-glass), while the rest of the ship is an opaque anti-alloy? If -1 has no photoreception, why would it need a transparent hatch? I get that this is not its purpose. But then&#8230;what <em>is</em> its purpose? To provide a convenient way for the Foubdation to see into it? I would recommend making the whole pod anti-glass, or figure out some way for the scientists to see the interior without using a convenient window and removing said window.</p>
<p>Also, there is enough space dust around the moon to cause considerably more damage to the vessel and the pod. And, unless the craft spontaneously appeared near the moon (which is improbable, as -1 had only a few provisions left to bring on to the pod), then it would have run into even more cosmic dust and cause even more damage. I am not sure how to fix this, sorry.</p>
<p>e: Kudos on the science, but when you take that route you need to commit and make sure it's air-tight (pun unintended).</p>

							</div>

								
